     The function rreeaallhhoossttnnaammee() converts _i_p to the corresponding host name.
     This is done by resolving _i_p to a host name and then ensuring that the
     host name resolves back to _i_p.

     _h_o_s_t must point to a buffer of at least _h_s_i_z_e bytes, and will always be
     written to by this function.

     If the name resolution does not work both ways or if the host name is
     longer than _h_s_i_z_e bytes, inet_ntoa(3) is used to convert _i_p to an ASCII
     form.

     If the string written to _h_o_s_t is _h_s_i_z_e bytes long, _h_o_s_t will not be NUL
     terminated.

